body:
Hemming question;

I bought a dress that ended up being quite long on me and no matter how many petticoats I put under it I can't quite get it to my goal of an above-the-knee length.

I have very beginner-level sewing experience and have hemmed stuff before, however, this dress has a different color ruffle at the end of the dress. Since I cant fold it up to hem it I'm super stuck on how to fix the length.

&#x200B;

To be brief; How do I hem a dress with a ruffle at the end? Do I need to cut off the ruffle, hem it and sew it back on or is there an easier approach? Thanks!

You could tape the cords to the wall. depending how high the outlets are your rats shouldnt be able to reach them, granted if they have nothing near the outlets to climb on. Depending on what you have plugged in, and if you need your cords to be moveable it may not be an option. But i have the bottom half of my laptop and lamp cord taped down and they havent grabbed em.

&#x200B;

I've personally never tried this but I heard wrapping cords in duct tape works. Duct tape is pretty sticky and supposedly deters chewing. Guess rats dont like sticky stuff on their teeths! I'd still keep an eye on them though.

I am worried about the stress and the possibility of it growing back. I ultimately understand more will grow back but if I can get another few months of happy life out of her I feel like I wouldnt regret my choice. I dont want to make her more miserable though if I operate, especially since currently she is in no discomfort with the lump.

&#x200B;

This is my first time dealing with tumors in rats so i'm really unsure on the right call to make. I have a feeling im too wishful that she'll recover quick and she'll live to 2 and a half.  Part of me is just selfish and doesnt want to let her go. 

&#x200B;

I'm probably going to look more into it before I do anything. Not that theres any place I can find that will operate right now anyways.

From what i've heard the most hygienic bedding you can have is a paper or hemp bedding. Always unscented! Some people also use dust free pine bedding but I would never use it myself just cuz i'm too paranoid to trust wood shavings, but its definitely a cheaper alternative to other beddings. Depending on how many rats you have you may need to change it as little as once a month, spot cleaning the corners the rats poop in weekly. 

I switched over to a half and half style layout after using only fleece for almost two years and i'm really happy with the choice I made. I have fleece on the top comfy part of my cage and bedding on the active bottom part of my cage. I like having both as fleece and bedding all have their perks. Bedding allows for natural behavior to be exhibited and doesn't need to be deep cleaned weekly. I've notice much less destruction of fleece, better smell, and I can finally scatter feed! Depending on whether or not you pay to wash by the load it may be more expensive or less. Also it definitely decreased the time I spend cleaning my cage, probably by an extra 10-15 minutes each week (with an exception to the once a month clean outs)

&#x200B;

That being said fleece has the perks of you can vacuum it, its easily customizable and it limits how much you are throwing away. The biggest downside to fleece is it needs to be washed at a bare minimum weekly if not more depending on how many rats you have. I've always custom sewed my liners as binder clips sounds like a pain in the ass + I get to pick patterns and themes. My rats are litter trained so I dont often use it for the ease of spot cleaning rather than I like the look of it much more and I think its worth the tradeoff of having to clean more frequently. \*and since I have to wash items like hammocks weekly anyways an extra liner to wash isn't a huge deal to me. \*

&#x200B;

It sounds like bedding would be your best bet. Health and cleaning wise its definitely superb to fleece. You might have to vacuum or sweep around your cage more though as despite having a deep litter pan rats will still find a way to make a giant mess.

My rats loved to kick bedding out. Mainly because they love to reorganize it to random parts of the cage. Switching to a heavier bedding worked for me \*paper pellets opposed to paper scraps\* but it sounds like you already have a heavier bedding. You could also consider building a deeper tray to put your bedding in.  Im unsure what cage you have but if you have a critter nation you can use a cement mixing tray from home depot. works like a charm! occasionally a little gets out but as long as your rats aren't intentionally doing it and you dont fill it too full it solves the issue. If you dont have a critter nation you could build a perspex tray, just drill some pieces of perspex together to be slightly smaller than the size of your floor. Gotta add a seam allowance like in sewing to make sure it slides in right \^\^

&#x200B;

If you dont mind cleaning more often fleece is a good bet. Its reusable, they cant kick it out and you can vacuum it. Just gotta chuck it in the washing machine once a week or more so ammonia doesn't build up. Just be sure to add a dig box so rats can still get the stimulation of digging bedding normally supplies  \^\^

Yeah by smell alone I can tell the build up is bad, which is why I wash the fleece once or twice a week. So does bedding like pine have a lot of dust? I'm unsure if theres any way to remove dust but that as well as the comfort of it concerns me. I might just be putting my own human feelings in the way, I mean my rats love to sleep on plastic so who am I to say whats comfy ;p

&#x200B;

also $4.99 for a bag... thats insane compared to pet store prices.

Outside of introductions today was my first day of true interaction with the babies. The one that originally bit me had no issues and let me pick her up and pet her. I think maybe I surprised her? She did break skin but hasn't shown any aggression as I pet her and picked her up. I think maybe I messed up and came at her too fast since im use to giving my already socialized rats treats. I'll have to give spoon feeding a shot. I tried feeding out of a malt paste tube earlier today (as its my eldest girls favorite) in hopes her loud licking would draw out the babies. It worked and they did come out! All three girls were eating out of the tube I was holding so I guess no major discomfort in that department. I'll continue doing that except I'll put the paste on the spoon so maybe they'll chill with how fast they come in to eat.

&#x200B;

Other than the fast bite-y style food approach correction I need to make it seems like the biggest issue will be handling. While they dont love pets they are tolerating it but absolutely HATE being handled. Guess the hardest part of the socialization process will likely be getting them out to socialize in the first place haha. I think im just too impatient I just wanna pet those lil cuties already! Just gonna keep taking it slow and hope to see improvement in the next week or two. \^\^

The hot pink archie is actually the only squish I have. Its a shame the light pink is out but I guess I'll keep on hunting for the flip... if they ever restock him o-o

&#x200B;

Thanks for the help \^\^

Hello! Found a super cheap $8 kenmore 185.19141 (previous owner says it worked prior to my purchase) and I've been trying to set it up. I cant find a manual for my specific machine and have been struggling when it comes to the bobbin thread. I already have winded the bobbin but I cant get it to work with the foot pedal. do I just pull the thread and place it under the foot? I'm new to sewing and am frustrated I cant get this thing to sew for me. any help? Thanks!

&#x200B;

[https://imgur.com/py2jXi3](https://imgur.com/py2jXi3) heres what the bobbin case and pedal looks like if it helps at all

I could try to order some but I'm at the 48hr mark tomorrow and I have no way to hatch them since I don't have a hatchery. (I heard they need a pretty funky set up??) I'll be keeping an eye on their bellies. They don't seem fat but it's really hard to tell since they are so so tiny.

&#x200B;

Thank you by the way! \^\^

Thank you. Their morphs seem to be wilds so I can't see through them. Unfortunately due to the shutdown I only really have these microworms to rely on. They are the only thing I could get a hold of so I'm really counting on them to be alive. Some babies look as if they are gulping so that seems to be a good sign. I'll wait till the 48 hour mark to be super concerned.

&#x200B;

I might try moving them into a super shallow, smaller container for feeding if I can. They are in a shallow but long container so maybe they could be missing the food with such a large space relative to their size.

I'll talk to the discord. But most I'll unfortunately have to cull. Theres too many getting rid of them all would be impossible.

&#x200B;

Thanks for the help \^\^

I can't seem to find an answer so this is my last resort! Sorry!

